`md list -r` must find a .md file in a subdirectory, not only the ones at the
top level. The fixture holds notes/one.md and notes/deeper/two.md, so the
answer is two files.

This case is also the reason the harness stages a subdirectory whole. Before
that, a fixture could only be a file, and no case could hand `list` a tree to
walk, so recursion went untested.

The output cannot be pinned byte for byte, because `list` prints the absolute
path of every file it finds and the scratch directory is named afresh on each
run. So the case asserts a property instead: `list` puts a blank line between
entries, so each file becomes one block, and the block count is the file
count.

Its pair is list-does-not-recurse-without-the-r-flag, which sees only one.
